##DESCRIPTION
## Derivatives problem, MAT237 C1 worksheet Q4
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('multivariable calculus', 'derivatives', 'velocity’)

## DBsubject('Multivariable Calculus')
## DBchapter('Derivatives')
## DBsection('Derivatives of one variable')
## Date('11/7/2023')
## Author('')
## Institution('University of Toronto')
## TitleText1('MAT237 Worksheets')
## EditionText1('')
## AuthorText1('')
## Section1('C1')
## Problem1('4')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserVectorUtils.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Vector")->variables->are(t => 'Real');

$g  = Compute("<t cos(t), t sin(t), t>");
$dg = $g->D('t');

$pos     = $g->eval(t => Compute("2 pi"));
$vel     = $dg->eval(t => Compute("2 pi"));
$tangent = $vel->unit;

BEGIN_PGML
Let [` g: \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^3 `] be given by [` g(t)=(t\cos t, t\sin t, t) `].

a) Compute the postion at [`2\pi`]. [_]{$pos}

b) Compute the velocity of [`g`] at [`2\pi`].  [_]{$vel}

c) Compute the unit tangent vector of [`g`] at [`2\pi`].  [_]{$tangent}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) For this, evaluate [`g`] at [`t=2\pi`] or  [`[$pos]`].

b) We first need the derivative.  This is 
[``` g'(t) = [$dg]```]
and then evaluate it at [`t=2\pi`] or [`g'(2\pi) = [$vel] `]. 

c) For this, divide through by the length of the vector in b) this is [`[$tangent]`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
